WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.929
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.929 --> 00:00:09.700
Last summer I was visiting family in
Singapore when I got a text message.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.820
Please respond to my mail of yesterday,
thanks.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.390
Not knowing the number, I responded
with the usual sorry, who is this?

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.460
Adam S comes the response.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:21.390
Thanks for not realizing.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.000
Thanks for not realizing?

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.400
That's obnoxious, but whatever.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.930
I'm going to be polite in here.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.400
So I respond with, sorry,
what's your full name?

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.320
I don't see any emails from you.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.940
To which he responds,
Adam loosing patience solar.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:41.560
Adam loosing patience solar.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:42.570
Are you serious?

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.850
Who are you?

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:45.840
I'm being patient with you.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.810
Your first strike was thanks for
not realizing.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.320
But this one's really pushing it.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.880
Where are your manners?

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.420
So I pull my phone, I'm frustrated and be
like, let me teach you how to spell losing

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.871
because you clearly didn't
go to primary school.

00:00:56.871 --> 00:01:02.920
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Instead, I paused and I showed

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.270
the text message to my younger brother
who read it and said, wait a minute.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.880
I think dad works for an Adam solar.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.340
And then it dawned on me.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.880
I was borrowing my Dad's SIM
card in Singapore.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.520
So I must have been texting his coworkers.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.140
So I run to Dad and show him the phone.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.060
Dad, I think I'm talking to
your to your friends there.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.060
And here's the conversation,
looks up and says poke it.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.510
I'm glad you're not the angry
poker that used to be.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.780
Otherwise today,
I'd be looking for a new job.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.040
This experience first
introduced me to the notion

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.100
that emotional intelligence is extremely
important in the business workplace.

00:01:43.855 --> 00:01:47.545
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It can literally save your job.

00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:48.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And turns out there's a lot of data to

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:49.605
support this.

00:01:49.605 --> 00:01:52.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Daniel Goleman, who writes about this

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:55.740
in the Harvard Business Review,
arrives at this conclusion.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.760
He says emotional
intelligence matters more and

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:59.590
more, the higher up you get in a company.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:04.613
In fact, the difference
between a star performer and

00:02:04.613 --> 00:02:09.390
an average performer is 90%
attributable to emotional intelligence.

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.700
Consider the following two examples
where a lack of emotional intelligence

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.390
was quite harmful.

00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:26.290
Uber CEO Travis Kalanick got in
a fight with an Uber driver on video.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:31.619
The Uber driver's scene telling
Kalanick though he lost $97,000 and

00:02:31.619 --> 00:02:35.300
went bankrupt because Uber changed
his policies and reduced its fares.

00:02:36.505 --> 00:02:38.080
Kalanick then yells back saying,

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.210
some people don't like to take
responsibility for their own shit.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.830
They blame everything on somebody else.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:48.011
This outburst cost Kalanick
personally $200,000 and

00:02:48.011 --> 00:02:50.930
eventually contribute
to him losing his job.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.470
Or consider Elon Musk.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.240
When British diver Vernon Unsworth
publicly criticized Elon Musk's idea on

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:05.440
twitter, Musk lashed back, calling
him a pedophile and a child rapist.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.270
This led to a prolonged midyear crisis and
a lawsuit.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:17.070
My point is that I should be
the CEO of Tesla and Uber.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:24.180
But in all seriousness, as we progress
in our careers, we'll be faced by

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:28.290
situations and people that test our
patience more and more frequently.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:30.790
And in those situations,

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.200
the cost of not handling that
with poise will also increase.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.750
So it's of paramount importance that
we work on our emotional intelligence

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:39.761
before we go out and change the world.

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:45.330
So you're probably wondering now,
how do I do this?

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.110
Well, there's two important parts
of the brain that need to be

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.940
trained sequentially.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.430
The first is the neocortex.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.600
The neocortex handles logic.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:59.480
So if you want to learn Excel, you read
a book, and now you know how to use Excel.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:00.120
It learns quickly.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.950
The limbic system handles emotions.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:09.580
This one's a little slower to teach
something to the limbic system.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.303
We need consistency or
practice over a long time.

00:04:13.303 --> 00:04:15.980
So how about we take
the first step together?

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:19.650
Let's train our neocortex and
let's take the example of anger.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:26.000
So, first, our aim here is to convince
the neocortex that in some situations,

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:26.970
anger is counterproductive.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.230
So in the case of the text messages,
when I get angry,

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:35.910
I'm excreting chemicals in my body that
are literally poisoning my system.

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.820
In other words,
I'm drinking the poison and

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.610
I'm hoping that the guy on the other
side of the text message gets hurt.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.705
So what does that neocortex say to this,
illogical.

00:04:46.705 --> 00:04:51.240
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the case of Travis Kalanick,

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.347
we need to understand that anger is not
helping him drive towards the objective.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.340
If anything,
it's pushing him further away.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:02.200
Not only did he fail to convince the Uber
driver of his side of the argument,

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.210
he also lost a lot of money and
eventually his job.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.390
What does the neocortex say to this again,
illogical.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:15.760
And in the case of Elon Musk, we need to
understand that if he lets every hater

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.210
online get him bothered, then boy,
he's going to live a terrible life.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:23.470
In this case, he may not have lost his
job, but he will definitely lose his peace

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.800
of mind everytime a bully picks up
the phone and fires off a tweet.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:32.580
So hopefully by now your neocortex is
convinced that in some situations,

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:33.420
anger is counterproductive.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:36.650
Now, comes the hard part.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:37.310
The limbic system.

00:05:39.657 --> 00:05:41.980
Because you can convince your neocortex or
something.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.640
But your limbic system will say,
I'm still angry.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:46.490
I still have anger.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.070
Now, I've been working on this for

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.725
a while you remember my dad
said I was an angry child.

00:05:51.725 --> 00:05:53.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.620
I found the PRO methodology
to work really well for me.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:58.749
The P in PRO stands for Pause.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:04.300
Pausing in a difficult situation gives
you time to act, instead of reacting.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.130
So when I got the text message, I was able
to pause, and gather more information,

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:14.130
and then act, as opposed to reacting
with an equally sassy message.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.590
The R in PRO stands for Re-frame.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:23.240
When Uber CEO Travis Kalanick
heard those words

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.760
he took that as a personal affront to him.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.710
Instead, he could have reframed
those words as product feedback

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:33.700
coming from a very important
company constituents, the drivers.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:38.030
Had he done that he could have
probably avoided the 2019 Uber strike

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.430
that happened right before the IPO.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.480
And the O in PRO, stands for Own.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:45.410
Own your emotions.

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:48.460
And in my opinion, the most important one.

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:54.100
Currently, we blame the external
circumstances for internal emotions.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:57.480
So we're constantly trying to fix the
external world to fix our internal world.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:02.580
But the reality is that our influence on
the external world is heavily limited.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.553
So own means to go from
an external locus of control for

00:07:06.553 --> 00:07:09.750
your emotions to an internal locus
of control of your emotions.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:13.400
So Elon Musk did not get
angry because somewhere

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:15.630
some person hated on him on Twitter.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:19.220
He got angry because internally
he was attached to the idea

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.700
that no one should
criticize him publicly and

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:26.615
by changing that expectation, he can
change his emotional state, one second.

00:07:28.889 --> 00:07:35.702
[SOUND] So how about we handle
this situation like a PRO.

00:07:35.702 --> 00:07:38.887
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:38.887 --> 00:07:43.989
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:43.989 --> 00:07:45.431
&gt;&gt; And pause,

00:07:45.431 --> 00:07:45.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:50.843 --> 00:07:53.490
&gt;&gt; Reframe.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:57.210
Maybe spilling juice all over myself
makes us more entertaining for you.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.805
And there'll be more views.

00:08:00.805 --> 00:08:05.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And own,

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:08.920
I am not feeling what I'm feeling right
now because I spilled juice over myself.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:15.115
It's because I had an expectation
internally that this talk go flawlessly.

00:08:15.115 --> 00:08:19.360
So my point is that when
in these situations,

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.600
anger is not the only thing
that can be worked on with PRO.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.860
You can also do this with disappointment,

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:28.320
with hatred, with sadness,
any other emotion.

00:08:29.770 --> 00:08:33.180
So I hope that you walk out of this room,
and next time you're faced with

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:38.090
a challenging situation, you evaluate
the merit of the emotional reaction.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:43.652
And then you pause, you reframe and
you own, thank you.

00:08:43.652 --> 00:08:46.908
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:46.908 --> 00:08:50.227
[MUSIC]

